<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<cfsSimulation xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
               xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation ../../../../../share/xml/CFS-Simulation/CFS.xsd"
               xmlns="http://www.cfs++.org/simulation">

  <documentation>
    <title>Coulomb friction: a fully sliding interface transmits exactly mu times the normal force</title>
    <authors><author>kroppert</author></authors>
    <date>2026-08-06</date>
    <keywords><keyword>contact</keyword></keywords>
    <references>none</references>
    <isVerified>no</isVerified>
    <description>
      Gate for the SLIP branch of the Coulomb return map, and the counterpart of
      ContactFrictionStick2d.

      A traction-driven model cannot gate slip: once the applied tangential load exceeds what
      friction can hold there is no static answer at all. So this test is DISPLACEMENT driven.
      The upper body is pushed sideways by a prescribed 1e-3 m on its top face while 1e6 Pa
      presses it down; its y support is still the contact interface alone.

      1e-3 m of imposed sliding against an elastic stick range of mu*p/eps_T = 1.1e-7 m puts
      every integration point four orders of magnitude outside the Coulomb cone, so all of them
      slip. The assertion is then fixed by the Coulomb law rather than by the elasticity
      solution:

      totalTangentialForce = mu * totalContactForce = 0.4 * 2e6 = 8e5 N/m

      with totalContactForce = p_N * L = 2e6 N/m fixed by equilibrium. Neither number depends on
      the imposed displacement, on tangentialPenalty, or on how the pressure is distributed --
      which is what makes it a test of the return map's projection onto the cone rather than of
      anything upstream. numStickPoints must be 0 and numSlipPoints 2.

      This is genuine sliding but not a violation of the small-sliding assumption: 1e-3 m is far
      less than the 2 m interface, so the pairing stays inside its primary element.
    </description>
  </documentation>

  <fileFormats>
    <input>
      <hdf5 fileName="ContactFrictionSlip2d.h5" linearizeEntities="__none__"/>
    </input>
    <output><hdf5/></output>
    <materialData file="mat.xml" format="xml"/>
  </fileFormats>

  <domain geometryType="plane">
    <regionList>
      <region name="master_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
      <region name="slave_elem" material="Aluminium"/>
    </regionList>
    <surfRegionList>
      <surfRegion name="master_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="slave_iface"/>
      <surfRegion name="top"/>
      <surfRegion name="bottom"/>
    </surfRegionList>
  </domain>

  <fePolynomialList>
    <Lagrange><gridOrder/></Lagrange>
  </fePolynomialList>

  <integrationSchemeList>
    <scheme>
      <method>Gauss</method>
      <order>4</order>
      <mode>absolute</mode>
    </scheme>
  </integrationSchemeList>

  <sequenceStep>
    <analysis><static/></analysis>

    <pdeList>
      <mechanic subType="planeStrain">
        <regionList>
          <region name="master_elem"/>
          <region name="slave_elem"/>
        </regionList>

        <contactList>
          <contactPair name="block_contact"
                       primarySurface="master_iface"
                       secondarySurface="slave_iface"
                       slidingType="small"
                       normalPenalty="100"
                       initialGapHandling="fromMesh"
                       integrationOrder="2">
            <friction type="coulomb" mu="0.4" tangentialPenalty="100"/>
          </contactPair>
        </contactList>

        <bcsAndLoads>
          <!-- lower body fully supported -->
          <fix name="bottom">
            <comp dof="x"/><comp dof="y"/>
          </fix>
          <!-- Drive the upper body sideways AT THE INTERFACE, not on its top face.
               Prescribing it on top would put the drag a full block height above the
               interface, and the resulting couple lifts one side of the block off - the
               interface then carries a moment, the pressure stops being uniform, and half
               the points open. Applying it at the interface itself removes the lever arm,
               so the pressure stays uniform and every point slips.
               Only x is prescribed: the y support is still the contact interface, which is
               what keeps totalContactForce an equilibrium statement. -->
          <displacement name="slave_iface">
            <comp dof="x" value="1e-3"/>
          </displacement>
          <pressure name="top" value="1e6"/>
        </bcsAndLoads>

        <storeResults>
          <nodeResult type="mechDisplacement"><allRegions/></nodeResult>
        </storeResults>
      </mechanic>
    </pdeList>

    <linearSystems>
      <system>
        <solutionStrategy>
          <standard>
            <setup idbcHandling="penalty"/>
            <matrix storage="sparseNonSym" reordering="noReordering"/>
            <nonLinear method="newton" logging="no">
              <lineSearch type="none"/>
              <!-- Judge convergence on the INCREMENT here, not on the residual.
                   StdSolveStep normalises the residual error by the norm of the LINEAR
                   right-hand side - the applied pressure alone - while both the friction
                   force (8e5 N/m) and the penalty reaction to the prescribed 1e-3 m of
                   sliding sit in the solution-dependent one. The relative residual therefore
                   parks at 0.43 and cannot go lower even though the iterate is exact: it
                   stops moving at 6e-15 from iteration 3 on, and every reported contact
                   quantity is stable from iteration 1.
                   This is a pre-existing openCFS normalisation limitation, not something
                   friction introduces. -->
              <incStopCrit>1e-9</incStopCrit>
              <resStopCrit>1</resStopCrit>
              <maxNumIters>20</maxNumIters>
            </nonLinear>
          </standard>
        </solutionStrategy>
        <solverList><directLU/></solverList>
      </system>
    </linearSystems>

  </sequenceStep>
</cfsSimulation>
